so today we're building this sort of diy  pet cam for tracking what your pets do  while you're gone  whether you want to know whether your  dog's hanging out on the couch or your  chickens are trespassing on the porch or  your cat's just doing some casual  software development while you're gone  we'll build an app that runs a machine  learning model on your phone to detect  events like  dog on couch and then sends you a  notification on slack  it'll also save snapshots of those  moments to the cloud so let's get to it  and build an ml-powered petcam that runs  on your smartphone  [Music]  one of the cool things about this  project is that we're going to do the  computer vision part entirely in a web  app that can run  on a smartphone it'll be privacy  conscious so we'll only send a  notification or save a picture to the  cloud when your pet does something that  you care about  otherwise your video and your photos  will stay entirely on your local device  i'm building this petcam with my  colleague jason mays a developer  advocate and host of the youtube show  made with tensorflow.js  hey jason thanks for having me dell you  know we've got a ton of old devices like  smartphones  just sitting around doing absolutely  nothing so i really wanted to think  about how we can give those a new life  in a way that anyone can then replicate  and hopefully use to do something useful  so why a pet cam yeah that's a great  question so essentially i saw a lot of  people around me these days  buying kittens and puppies and i think  it'd be quite fun to kind of  track those as they're growing up to  make sure we're not being too naughty  when you're leaving the house  when they're alone and of course you  know you can always extend this to other  objects in the future too as well  like a chicken yeah like a chicken as  well anything could be possible  all right let's do it okay so our app's  gonna work like this  first we'll build a pet tracking camera  app that runs on your phone and detects  common pets like cats and dogs  then we'll make our app tracker even  more powerful by training a custom pet  model on google cloud  to detect chickens and finally we'll  connect our front end to a back end  built in firebase so that we can store  data and send messages to slack  okay so let's talk about the actual  front-end application  that tracks animals the first step is to  enable the webcam access  and load a tensorflow.js model for  object detection  today we'll use the coco ssd model that  recognizes 80 common household objects  in any picture that you pass to it now  importing tensorflow.js  and the machine learning model is  actually really simple  first you've got two script tag imports  for tensorflow.js and the model itself  next we can grab a reference to the  video object that we're rendering our  webcam to  and once we've got that we can call  cocosd.load we can then call  model.detect  and pass it the video element on our  webpage to classify the current frame  being played from our webcam so once we  can detect what's in a given video frame  we then need to send an alert when the  pet in this example a dog  gets close to an object that we care  about such as a food bowl  couch or bed here we simply allow the  user to choose from the objects the  model can recognize  to make their selection as shown and we  ask the user to ensure the device is set  up  in front of the object we want to  monitor once enabled  then when we've detected that the user  has placed the device in view of the  object they want to monitor  we can continuously check the video  stream to see if a chosen pet ever gets  close to that object  to do that we calculated the distance  between their bounding boxes  if the distance is less than some  threshold we'll send an alert to a back  end to then send a notification  now we also had to add some logic to  avoid spamming the user with too many  notifications in case the dog came in  and out of frame continuously  which you can learn all about over in  our blog posts so check the link in the  description after  and that's essentially how the front end  works if you want to play this yourself  and grab the code  you can find it over on glitch.com as  shown so after jason built the front end  we could start testing the app  but the problem was that jason and i  actually don't own any pets so we had to  get kind of creative with our testing  setups  and it also borrowed benji for corgi  from a friend to check  couch and board alerts for dogs which  caught him every single time as you can  see  it actually worked better than i  expected but then in the middle of  working on this project  i actually did acquire some pets two  baby chicks  but despite how incredibly adorable they  were our pre-built tensorflow model  didn't really recognize them  so i had to figure out a way to adopt  petcam to work for my chicks  which brings us to part two training a  custom chicken detection model  i did this using vertex ai google's new  machine learning development platform  look at these cute little data points  automl vision is a tool within the  vertex ai platform that lets you build a  vision model without writing any code  and then export it to tensorflow.js  first in the vertex ai console i created  a new image object detection data set  then i uploaded about 100 pictures of my  new baby chicks to google cloud storage  so they could be imported into vertex ai  after the data imported i opened an  image and annotated it by hand  i decided to train the model to  recognize moira and millie separately  because they're two different breeds of  chicken and i thought it would be super  cool if the model could tell them apart  i also annotated the position of their  food and water bowl so i could calculate  when the chicks were eating and drinking  then i just did this a hundred more  times and it took about 30 minutes  when i was done labeling i hit train new  model and then selected automl edge to  create a model optimized for an edge  device  i named my model selected some  configuration options  configured it to train for five node  hours and then waited  when my model was done at training i  checked out the precision and recall  in the evaluate tab and this model was  pretty accurate  to actually use it in my app go to the  deploy and test tab click tensorflow.js  and then choose a location in cloud  storage to export the model from here we  can download it and use it right in our  app's javascript front end  all right now let's connect the back end  to make this app actually useful  we want to save these pet events when  they occur and then set an alert to the  user over slack  for this i of course turn to my favorite  serverless toolkit firebase  when the front-end app detects the dog's  on the couch it saves a snapshot and  some metadata about the event to  firestore then i built a little app a  front-end  to pull data from that firestore  database and then display it as a sort  of  diary so you can keep track of all the  cute or mischievous things that your pet  is doing while you're gone  to set up slack notifications i used a  firebase cloud function to call the  slack api  it's set up with the trigger so that  when data is written to the firestore  database it automatically triggers my  firebase cloud function to run  and then send an alert and that's it  that's the whole back end  if you want to learn how to build a pet  cam yourself check out the code and the  blog post linked below  you can also check out tensorflow.js and  other primo models you can use on our  websites  and of course if you want more  inspiration check out made with tfjs  hashtags on twitter and linkedin  by the way if you want to see how my  colleague mark who built his very own  pet cam that runs on device as well  using coral then check out his take on a  very similar project here  i guess great minds just think alike or  something and if you do try this  yourself make sure you let us know in  the comments  see you next time  you
 

